CHROMIUM: netfilter: make xt_quota2 counters per-network-namespace

xt_quota2 keeps one global counter list and creates its procfs
directory once, in init_net:

	static struct proc_dir_entry *proc_xt_quota;
	proc_xt_quota = proc_mkdir("xt_quota", init_net.proc_net);

Every counter file therefore lands in the host's /proc/net/xt_quota no
matter which netns installed the rule. The fydroid (Android 13)
container runs in its own netns, so netd's

	iptables -m quota2 --name globalAlert

succeeds but materialises the counter in the host, and netd's later
BandwidthController::updateQuota() ->
fopen("/proc/net/xt_quota/globalAlert", "we") gets ENOENT inside the
container. procfs cannot create a file on open, so this never
recovers. NetworkStatsService then fails boot phase 550 and takes
system_server down in a permanent crash loop
("FATAL EXCEPTION IN SYSTEM PROCESS: NetworkStats"), which kills every
app launch.

Move the counter list, its lock and the procfs directory into per-netns
state via register_pernet_subsys(), matching what upstream did for the
other named-counter xtables matches. Each netns gets its own
/proc/net/xt_quota, owned by uid/gid 0 of that netns' user namespace so
the entries resolve to the container's root instead of overflowuid
(unchanged global root for init_net).

quota2_net_exit() runs before quota_mt2_destroy() during netns
teardown, so it clears ->proc_xt_quota under the lock and drops the
subtree in one shot; destroy() snapshots the pointer and skips its
per-counter remove_proc_entry() when the directory is already gone.

This is the same port already carried by chromeos-kernel-6_12 as
070-port-xt-quota2.patch, minus that patch's v6.12-only API churn
(strlcpy/strscpy, linux/atomic.h) which does not apply to v6.1. The
module init/exit error paths gained the missing netlink_kernel_release()
along with it.

